              },
              "question": "<p>In our last T/F question, we confirmed that anything that is absorbed from the GIT, from the middle portion of the oesophagus to the middle portion of the rectum must go through the portal venous system and through the liver before reaching systemic circulation. <strong>Given this, what would you expect to happen when a client pushes a rectal suppository 'too far'?</strong></p>\n"
            },
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "metadata": {
              "contentType": "Multiple Choice",
              "license": "U",
              "title": "Untitled Multiple Choice"
            },
            "subContentId": "e1402f54-e67e-4cfa-b706-5ea0de787dfc"
          },
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Lipophilic (oil based) drugs often access a protein carrier molecule so that they can be carried through the circulation. \n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Each drug is different as to the % of the drug is bound to a carrier protein. Sometimes it is a high protein binding (for instance, 99%) and other times it is a low protein binding (for instance, 10%)\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Only the unbound portion of the drug (the drug that is not bound to the proteins) is active and it is only the unbound portion of the drug that can be metabolised. \n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The statement was incorrect.&nbsp;As the unbound portion of the drug moves into the cells to be metabolised or to perform its function, the equilibrium changes, and an equal amount of drug disassociates from the protein carrier. If you do not understand this, please view the video again. </div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed, the statement was incorrect.&nbsp;As the unbound portion of the drug moves into the cells to be metabolised or to perform its function, the equilibrium changes, and an equal amount of drug disassociates from the protein carrier. </div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "The % of the drug that is bound to proteins will always remain bound to the proteins until it is degraded in the body \n"
                }
              ],
